From 8c50b583a33c8b1063aec9c8fa9635671b69a9d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 01:18:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] terminal processes survive a gateway restart in
 their own scope and reattach

t_01a1c7d04d9046d2 (part C of t_01a1c7c587142bb7). A hub core update restarts the gateway/backend
unit (KillMode=control-group): every terminal child in its cgroup died with it, e.g. a background
quick_check on 2026-11-10. persist_on_release jobs already left the unit and were re-attached;
ordinary background jobs and long foreground commands were not.

New config key terminal.update_survivable_processes (default false; Linux with a usable
systemd-run --user --scope only):
- background=true jobs default to persist_on_release (explicit false wins; pty=true jobs keep their
  PTY): own scope, file-backed log + exit file, re-attached by pid + start time by the next
  backend, completion delivered once.
- foreground commands with an effective timeout > 60 s run in their own scope with output teed to
  such a log, held in processes.json while the tool waits. An interrupt with tool_reason
  "hermes update pause" / "gateway shutdown" / "backend exit" converts the command into a
  registered background job (notify on exit) and the result ends with
  "process continues as <session_id>". A user Stop, timeout or any other interrupt kills it as
  before; a yield-to-background reuses the same scope/log.
- key on without a scope: today's behaviour, one WARNING.

Proof (hermes-hub, real transient user service stopped like the gateway):
tests/tools/test_update_survivable_processes.py 13/13 GREEN; with the wiring reverted 6 FAIL
(background job dead after the owner stop, foreground command killed). Key-off neighbours
(persist/readopt/registry/terminal/interrupt/yield suites) 232 passed.
